      "problem": "Solve the inequality $3 \\cdot 5^x <   7^{3-x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {5}^{x} & <{7}^{3-x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot {5}^{x}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{3-x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{3-x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(3-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <3\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 & <3\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7) & <3\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x & <\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
